  const project = await createTestProject();
  const store = await openRunStore(project.directory);
  await store.createRun("recover");
  const host = createFakePluginContext(project.directory);
  const dispose = await RalphPlugin.setup(host.context);
  try {
    expect(host.statuses).toHaveLength(0);
    for (let i = 0; i < 100 && !host.statuses.length; i++) await Bun.sleep(5);
    expect(host.statuses).toHaveLength(1);
  } finally {
    await dispose?.();
    await project.cleanup();
  }
}, 15_000);
# Package validation — 2026-10-15

This is local implementation evidence, not cross-platform release certification. Exact SDK mapping is in [compatibility](opencode-compatibility.md); installation and the repeatable demo are in [README](../README.md).

## Reproduce

Use Bun 1.4.3, install from `bun.lock`, then run:

```sh
bun run typecheck
bun run lint
bun test tests/integration/15-package.test.ts
bun run build
bun run test:browser
bun run test:smoke
bun test tests/e2e/full-workflow.test.ts
bun run test:package
```

`test:package` builds and packs locally, installs the tarball into two committed temporary Git repositories with spaces, then loads installed runtime exports. All eight bundled skills and dashboard HTML/JS must survive packing. A stale runtime-log canary must not survive the clean build. The package allowlist excludes source, tests, fixture data, developer configuration and logs. Tests never publish, push, create a PR or merge feature changes into the target branch.

The installed public router discovers/approves/verifies commands, interviews, generates a PRD and issue graph in separate sessions, approves exact revisions, prepares an isolated baseline and schedules an issue. The controllable adapter supplies agent output and correlated human responses. Executor jobs run a real failing test, implement addition, run the passing test and commit; merger jobs integrate into candidates and approved commands validate them. The host explicitly calls `reviewFeature` after scheduling with functional lifecycle/tool options. Independent reviewers consume installed skills. No execution evidence is seeded.

The first report is `verified`. The second repository changes an approved environment reference after setup to trigger a real check timeout during baseline. That omission persists through integration and report as `incomplete-validation`; setup itself must succeed. Each consumer has separate command configuration, durable state, write/active-run locks and dashboard data. Dashboards coexist on allocated ports. The installed process ownership API is exercised with literal argv (spaces, quotes, shell characters and Unicode), app readiness and cleanup.

Temporary repositories are removed even on failure. `PACKAGE_EVIDENCE_PATH` retains JSON with OS, architecture, Bun/OpenCode versions, workflow stages, outcomes and remaining blockers. CI runs typecheck, lint, integration, browser, installed smoke and workflow on macOS, Ubuntu and native Windows and uploads the JSON per platform. Missing or failed platform results block release.

## Red/green evidence

- The new packaged stage regression failed because the old seeded demo had no setup/planning/executor/merger evidence. It passes through installed public commands with real Git/TDD work.
- Exercising that handoff exposed a production reporting defect: the scheduler's provisional `incomplete` flag remained after both reviewers passed, making a fully checked feature permanently incomplete. The scheduler records an explicit `reviewPending` marker. The coordinator qualifies that marker only for approved, fully integrated, nonempty passing checks bound to the reviewed commit, with no omissions or pending integration; an arbitrary incomplete attestation cannot be promoted. The installed passing/skipped pair verifies both outcomes. Existing review/recovery coverage is retained.
- The skipped fixture initially timed out during command setup and correctly failed readiness. The fixture now changes its approved environment reference only after setup; production setup-timeout behavior is preserved.
- Existing package regressions cover accidental developer-local self-dependencies, stale runtime logs, missing assets, ownership API exports and deferred startup recovery notifications.

Tests for implementation behavior use public entrypoints. Documentation and CI metadata updates are non-behavior changes; no tests restate their text or YAML.

## Tested scope and release blockers

Local platform: macOS arm64. Bun 1.4.3 (`c6da4a4d3`); OpenCode CLI/client/plugin/schema 2.0.29; TypeScript 5.9.3; Biome 2.4.6; Playwright 1.56.1. Current command results are recorded below.

- Native Linux and Windows: CI configured; not executed in this local session. Their process trees, shells, paths and browser results remain unverified here.
- Credentialed provider session: gated by `OPENCODE_LIVE_SMOKE=1`, endpoint, model and authentication; skipped without those inputs. Fake-agent or local-model results do not establish provider compatibility.
- Complete credentialed setup-to-report: unavailable because production `requestUserInput` has no verified human provenance. The deterministic host supplies this boundary explicitly; it cannot certify a working production approval channel.
- Automatic final review: scheduling starts code review only. A host must provide functional environment/surfaces/tools to `reviewFeature`. The demo tests that public handoff; it does not imply automatic production dual-review wiring.
- Interactive TUI rendering and live owned-service lifecycle still need native host validation. Runtime remains attached to OpenCode lifetime.

