# OpenCode compatibility

## Supported combination

This package targets **OpenCode 2.0.29**. Its server/TUI definitions and typed adapter use the v2 API; v1 hosts are no longer supported. Version 0.2.0 replaces the earlier 1.19.0 integration.

| Component | Exact version |
| --- | --- |
| `@opencode/cli` (development/test CLI) | 2.0.29 |
| `@opencode/plugin`, `@opencode/client`, `@opencode/schema` | 2.0.29 |
| Bun | 1.4.3 |
| TypeScript / Biome | 5.9.3 / 2.4.6 |

The real-server smoke probe builds, packs, installs and loads the package in an isolated Git repository. On macOS arm64 it exercises completed sessions, direct commands without model calls, event subscription, denied shell execution, linked-worktree observation restrictions, persistence, restart, explicit resume, interruption and status RPC. The deterministic local model proves the host boundary, not credentialed external-provider behavior. Native Linux/Windows results remain pending CI.

## Installation and entry points

Follow [README](../README.md). Configure `plugins` with the installed **package directory**, preferably a canonical, encoded file URL. Do not use the old `plugin` entry pointing to `dist/plugin.js`. OpenCode resolves `server.js` and `tui.js` from that directory; both have default definitions with `id: 'ralph'` and `setup(context)`. Package imports also expose `/server` and `/tui`.

Tests use `node_modules/@opencode/cli/bin/opencode.exe`, including on macOS/Linux. The manifest trusts this pinned CLI's postinstall script. Version changes require new runtime evidence, not just a dependency bump.

V2 activates external plugins asynchronously. Wait for command registration before dispatching external jobs; otherwise initial recovery may encounter newly recorded job intent. Local configuration changes require host activation/reload to complete.

Command registration can be visible before `plugin.list` publishes the active plugin. The installed-package probe also waits for that inventory entry before checking server/TUI features. This preserves the supported v2 mapping described in the [official plugin documentation](https://opencode.ai/v2/docs/build/plugins/); the ticket's proposed `@opencode-ai/plugin` import belongs to v1.

## Session adapter

`src/opencode/adapter.ts` accepts a narrow `AdapterHost`; `createAdapterClient` supplies it over the real v2 client and Ralph RPC. The plugin supplies the host's native session domain. V2 calls use flattened options and throw typed/transport failures rather than returning v1 `{ data, error, response }` envelopes.

```ts
const client = createAdapterClient(url, undefined, {
  Authorization: `Basic ${btoa('opencode:' + password)}`,
});
const session = await client.session.create({
  location: { directory }, title,
  model: { providerID, id: modelID },
});
const receipt = await client.session.prompt({ sessionID: session.id, text });
await client.session.wait({ sessionID: session.id });
const history = await client.session.context({ sessionID: session.id });
await client.session.interrupt({ sessionID: session.id });
await client.session.command({ sessionID: session.id, name: 'ralph-status', text: runId });
const commands = await client.command.list({ location: { directory } });
for await (const event of client.event.subscribe({ signal })) { /* v2 event.data */ }
```

Job intent is durable before session creation; the returned session ID is durable before prompting. Workspace selection occurs at session creation; subsequent calls target that session ID. Model fields map from Ralph's `{ providerID, modelID }` contract to v2 `{ providerID, id }`.

Prompt acknowledgment is not completion. The adapter waits, finds the delivered user message with the receipt's ID, and checks the subsequent turn. Success requires completed assistant text, `finish: 'stop'`, a successful idle record and no failed tools or session errors. Unexpected subsequent input makes correlation uncertain and returns missing output. Earlier failed/denied tools, including caught code-mode failures in tool metadata, cannot be erased by later assistant success.

Cancellation is recorded before interruption. Journals retain the existing `session.abort` operation kind for compatibility with persisted state; v2 uses `session.interrupt` underneath. A late-created cancelled session is interrupted without prompting. Stale results cannot update superseded attempts.

## Commands, observations and notifications

Commands register with `context.command.transform`; tools register with `context.tool.transform`. Status/pause/resume/stop execute directly. The exact command output is queued as a synthetic inbox item with `resume: false`, and a status event notifies the TUI without waking the model. Pending output can be inspected with `client.session.inbox.list`; it is consumed on the next session execution. Status never depends on generated assistant text.

V2 does not expose the plugin tool catalog as a public client endpoint. `src/opencode/bridge.ts` defines location-scoped Ralph RPC for tool discovery, observation preparation and status notification. Review sessions expose only `ralph_review_*` aliases of permitted observation tools. Aliases retain each original tool's permission action and execution function. Context filtering hides mutation tools and code-mode `execute`; the execution hook also rejects unapproved calls. Normal sessions retain their original tools and hide the observation aliases. Host denials are never changed to allows and no permissions are automatically approved.

The TUI entry listens for `rpc.ralph.status` in its location and shows an informational toast; cleanup unsubscribes. Server-only consumers can subscribe to status RPC events and use the durable status/dashboard. Interactive TUI rendering has unit contract coverage; native interactive/platform validation remains pending.

## Recovery and remaining limits

The canonical Git common directory owns `<git-common-dir>/ralph/runs`. Its fsynced JSONL journal remains authoritative; snapshots use atomic rename and writers use ownership-verified locks. Linked worktrees share state. Multiple v2 locations in one server recover that state once and only the last location's cleanup stops shared work. Restart still requires explicit resume and never resets attempts or review budgets. No new state schema or migration is needed for existing journals.

V2 exposes session forms, but Ralph's human-input/approval provenance workflow is still absent. `requestUserInput` remains explicitly unsupported; neither assistant text nor API-created user messages establish human approval. The command setup workflow now validates correlated host-supplied approvals and lifecycle execution in fixtures, with production execution permissions delegated to `ctx.permission.assert`. Production human approval remains unsupported. Deterministic installed-package coverage now includes setup, interview/PRD/graph approval, baseline, issue execution/integration and explicit dual-review coordination. The scheduler automatically starts code review only; functional lifecycle options must be supplied by a host. See [README](../README.md) and [package validation](package-validation.md).

## Reproduce

```sh
bun install --frozen-lockfile
bun run typecheck
bun run lint
bun run test
OPENCODE_SMOKE=1 bun run test:smoke
bun run test:package
```

On PowerShell set `$env:OPENCODE_SMOKE='1'` before running the smoke suite. The local probe uses an isolated test-only Basic-auth password. The separate live probe requires `OPENCODE_LIVE_SMOKE=1`, `OPENCODE_SMOKE_URL`, `OPENCODE_SMOKE_MODEL=provider/model` and the server's `OPENCODE_SMOKE_PASSWORD`; without its opt-in/endpoint/model it is skipped. Do not put credentials in package resources or fixture data.

## Issue 1 verification — 2026-10-14

- macOS arm64, Bun 1.4.3: typecheck and lint passed; all 19 issue-1 integration tests passed.
- `OPENCODE_SMOKE=1 bun test tests/smoke/opencode.test.ts`: **2 passed, 1 skipped**. The fresh-source install uses only the manifest/lockfile and an empty package cache. The installed-package probe builds, packs and loads Ralph in real OpenCode 2.0.29, correlates its local model response through the public router/adapter, preserves permission denial, observes events and verifies status after host restart.
- Red/green evidence: the empty-cache install failed with `ENOENT extracting tarball from opencode-ralph-plugin` before removing the accidental self-dependency from the manifest and regenerating the lockfile. It then passed. A warm cache had concealed the missing archive. Package installation must not depend on a previously built copy of itself.
- The real probe initially failed on an unpublished plugin inventory entry; the probe now waits for active inventory independently of command registration, retaining its TUI assertion.
- Fresh-install checks are gated because they download dependencies; the deterministic issue-1 suite requires neither network model access nor paid calls. Credentialed external-provider execution was skipped. Linux and Windows have CI jobs but were not run here; interactive TUI and human-response provenance remain unverified/unsupported as described above.

## Packaged workflow verification — 2026-10-15

macOS arm64, Bun 1.4.3 and the pinned OpenCode 2.0.29 combination passed the installed host smoke (3 passed across the smoke suite, 1 credentialed-provider skip), complete deterministic two-consumer setup-to-report test, six Chromium checks, typecheck/lint and the integration suite (194 passed, 1 separately gated browser smoke skipped). The demo performs real Git/TDD/integration and a real timeout omission; human responses and agent prose are supplied at the public adapter boundary. Full results, reproducible commands and remaining production/native-platform blockers are recorded in [package validation](package-validation.md). No Linux, native Windows or credentialed full-workflow success is claimed by these local results.
# Installation and operational troubleshooting

Use the exact dependency versions listed in [compatibility](opencode-compatibility.md) and Bun from the package manifest (`.bun-version` in a source checkout). Run the package probe from the source checkout: `bun run test:package`.

| Symptom | Check or action |
| --- | --- |
| Tarball install mentions a developer's home directory | Rebuild the current manifest; it no longer contains a self-dependency. Copy the archive into the consumer and install `./opencode-ralph-plugin-0.2.0.tgz`. Avoid percent-encoded absolute tarball URLs with Bun 1.4.3. |
| Plugin fails to load on a path with spaces or Windows | Generate the canonical package-directory URL with `realpathSync` and `pathToFileURL` using the README command. Use the encoded URL for plugin loading; do not hand-build drive-letter file URLs. |
| Slash command missing | Restart the pinned OpenCode server in the correct repository and inspect `plugins` in its local `opencode.json`. V2 activates external plugins asynchronously; allow activation to finish. All six Ralph commands are registered. Setup/orchestrator wait for correlated human responses; the production adapter currently reports unsupported provenance. |
| Global OpenCode behaves differently | Use OpenCode 2.0.29. This package uses the v2 plugin contract; v1 is no longer supported. |
| Local test CLI says postinstall was not run | Run `bun pm trust @opencode/cli`; the manifest trusts only this pinned CLI install script. |
| HTTP SDK returns 401 | Pass the v2 server Basic-auth header to `createAdapterClient(url, fetch, headers)`. The smoke test uses an isolated test-only password. |
| Dashboard inaccessible | Get a fresh URL from `/ralph-status`. Use its loopback host and fragment token. Tokens are process-scoped and change on server restart. The listener stops when the plugin is disposed. |
| Dashboard returns 401/403/405 | Authentication is required for API reads. Cross-origin and mutation requests are rejected. Use OpenCode for controls. |
| Reopened workflow does not continue | Recovery intentionally waits for `/ralph-resume`. Read the recovery facts first; resolve blocked/conflicting Git, session or ownership evidence. Attempts and revision budgets do not reset. |
| Writer lock or process ownership cannot be inspected | Do not delete state or kill arbitrary PIDs. Recovery only reclaims provably dead local owners. Native Windows requires PowerShell/CIM and `taskkill.exe`; POSIX requires `ps` and process-group signals. Unknown ownership is a visible blocker. Network-filesystem lock atomicity is not promised. |
| Completion says incomplete/unverified | Read skipped checks, unavailable criteria/review, failed cleanup and retained resources in the report. A successful assistant response does not validate checks. |
| Failed worktree remains | Keep it with its evidence for diagnosis. Do not remove a worktree referenced by an active operation. Successful issue cleanup is owned by integration, not package installation. |
| Live smoke is skipped | The credentialed probe requires opt-in, endpoint, model and v2 server authentication documented in README. A deterministic local model or fake adapter cannot prove provider compatibility. |
| Linux or Windows result is unavailable | Run the corresponding native CI matrix job. Missing or failed supported-platform evidence remains a release blocker. macOS results cannot certify Windows cleanup behavior. |

No install or release script pushes, publishes, creates a PR or merges a feature into its target. The two-repository demonstration uses local temporary Git branches only. Runtime state is under the Git common directory; logs, repositories, test fixtures and secrets are excluded from the package's allowlisted contents. The build removes stale `dist` output before compilation.
# Local package distribution

## Summary

Clean builds pack the runtime, dashboard and reviewer resources into a locally installable tarball. Tests install it in two real Git consumers and import the installed entry point and public modules, rather than certifying source imports as packaging evidence.

## Key behavior

- Version 0.2.0 targets OpenCode 2.0.29 and ships root `server.js`/`tui.js` entry points. Configure the package directory in `plugins`, not a compiled file in the v1 `plugin` list.
- `build` removes stale `dist` output; the manifest allowlists runtime resources and installation docs. Developer-local self-dependencies and runtime log canaries are regression checked.
- Fixture repositories have paths with spaces, relative tarball dependencies, distinct stores/locks and dashboard listeners. The installed process wrapper is tested for argv, readiness and tree cleanup.
- Real-server restart exposed a reentrant initialization deadlock. `src/plugin.ts` now defers recovery status events until initialization returns; the durable recovery summary is recorded synchronously. Disposal cancels pending notifications. Never await an SDK request into the host during plugin startup.
- `process` exposes the existing ownership API for approved command owners. It does not discover or approve commands.
- The deterministic installed demo drives setup, approved planning, real Git/TDD execution/integration and explicit dual review, and checks `verified` versus `incomplete-validation`. Its passing fixtures do not certify a complete product workflow or a live provider.
- CI runs all suites on macOS, Linux and native Windows without exclusions. Credentialed session smoke stays separately gated; production approval provenance and automatic functional-review wiring remain blockers.

## Code references

- `src/plugin.ts` — non-blocking startup notifications, covered by the deferred-notification regression in issue 15.
- `package.json`, `scripts/clean-build.ts` — clean build, resource allowlist and exports.
- `tests/helpers/packaged-workflow.ts` — installed public setup-to-report fixture, real TDD/Git and timeout omission.
- `src/core/types.ts`, `src/execution/scheduler.ts` — optional `reviewPending` distinguishes provisional scheduler state from missing validation attestation.
- `src/review/coordinator.ts` — qualifies explicit `reviewPending` scheduler validation only with approved, integrated, commit-bound passing checks and no omissions.
- `tests/helpers/package.ts`, `tests/helpers/packaged-probe.ts` — isolated tarball consumers and installed runtime assertions.
- `tests/integration/15-package.test.ts`, `tests/smoke/package-install.test.ts`, `tests/e2e/full-workflow.test.ts` — bounded regression/smoke/demo entrypoints.
- `scripts/package-smoke.ts`, `.github/workflows/ci.yml` — portable demo and platform evidence artifacts.
- `README.md`, `docs/package-validation.md` — installation, reproducible demo and exact evidence/limitations.

## Related

- [Workflow controls and recovery](workflow-recovery.md)
- [Bounded review and repair](bounded-review-repairs.md)

## Notes

Native Linux/Windows and credentialed full-workflow results remain unverified. The host must supply correlated human responses and functional review environment/tool options. No execution evidence is seeded. The integration suite allows 30 seconds per test for multi-repository Git fixtures; product timeouts are unchanged.

